{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQiCna9KTQdkwrnMYcdW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01M6nhKLk6uVKmeD3iYEGREA","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2569977521/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19686,"cache_read_input_tokens":27245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19686},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"68a63b42-96de-4f23-b9a6-2868a2391c36","timestamp":"2026-11-26T01:58:22.096Z","request_id":"req_011ChQiCnNjcvQkKm3wkSJ3R","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01M6nhKLk6uVKmeD3iYEGREA":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2569977521/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M6nhKLk6uVKmeD3iYEGREA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tslug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n3\tstatus: in-progress\n4\tcreated_at: 2026-11-23\n5\tcompleted_at:\n6\tsupersedes:\n7\tdepends_on: \n8\tremediates:\n9\tpriority: 4\n10\tcomponents: pose-mcp\n11\ttask_type: feature\n12\tsurface: standard\n13\tdelivers: capability:review-declared-threat-model\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Intent\n19\t\n20\t### Goal\n21\tLet a spec declare the accepted residual limits of a guarantee it makes, and let the review brief treat findings inside those limits as follow-ups to classify, not blockers to chase.\n22\t\n23\t### Business value\n24\tEach of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n25\t\n26\t### Constraints\n27\tA declared limit never hides a defect inside the declared scope. The reviewer may dispute a limit; the dispute is a finding with its own classification. Declared limits are visible in the closeout and the attestation.\n28\t\n29\t### Non-goals\n30\tLimiting the number of review rounds.\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Requirements\n33\t\n34\t### Functional\n35\t- R1: A spec shall be able to declare `### Accepted limits` entries with stable ids (`L<N>`), each naming the assumption and why it is accepted.\n36\t- R2: The review brief shall list the declared limits and instruct the reviewer to classify a finding that falls inside one as a follow-up naming the limit, or to dispute the limit explicitly.\n37\t- R3: The conclusion and the attestation shall record which findings were classified under which limit.\n38\t- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n39\t\n40\t### Non-functional\n41\tExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n42\t\n43\t### Security\n44\tNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n45\t\n46\t### Compatibility\n47\tRecords and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n48\t\n49\t## 3. Technical Plan\n50\t\n51\t### Affected areas\n52\t`pose-mcp` (CLI and engine), the manual and its embedded copy.\n53\t\n54\t### Artifacts\n55\t- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n56\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n57\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n58\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\n59\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go\n60\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go\n61\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\n62\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n63\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n64\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n65\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n66\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n67\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n68\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n69\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n70\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n71\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n72\t- modified: .pose/policy/delegation.json\n73\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n74\t- modified: POSE.md\n75\t- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n76\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n77\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n78\t\n79\t\n80\t### Delivery targets\n81\t\n82\t- capability:review-declared-threat-model module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n83\t\n84\t\n85\t### Accepted limits\n86\t- L1: The limits are written by the implementer, so a limit can be broader than it should be. Accepted because the brief presents them as data to verify and a disputed limit is a blocking finding of its own.\n87\t- L2: A spec edited after sealing changes its limits only through a new bundle. Accepted because the limits enter the plan digest, so an edit stales the review as any other change does.\n88\t\n89\t### API/contract changes\n90\tA spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n91\t\n92\t\n93\t### Data/storage changes\n94\t`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n95\t\n96\t\n97\t### Technical risks\n98\tA limit could be used to wave a real defect through. It is the spec's own declaration, sealed into the plan digest and visible in the brief and the attestation; the reviewer may dispute it with a finding of its own, which then blocks as any finding does.\n99\t\n100\t\n101\t## 4. Tasks\n102\t\n103\t### Planning\n104\t- [ ] Confirm the requirements against the current engine before starting.\n105\t\n106\t### Implementation\n107\t- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n108\t\n109\t### Validation\n110\t- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n111\t\n112\t## 5. Decisions\n113\t\n114\t### Decision D1\n115\t- Basis: R1, R2, R3\n116\tA finding inside a declared limit is neither an open finding nor an accepted risk: it gets its own disposition, `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity, requires a sealed limit and a reason, and records the limit's id. Accepted risks keep their severity bound; a limit is the spec's statement of scope, decided before the review.\n117\t\n118\t### Decision D2\n119\t- Basis: R1, R4\n120\tThe limits are sealed in the plan and the bundle, and in the plan digest, so the brief and the conclusion read them from what was reviewed, never from the current spec; both fields are omitted when empty so no earlier plan or bundle changes digest.\n121\t\n122\t## 6. Validation\n123\t\n124\t### Strategy\n125\tEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n126\t\n127\t### Deterministic checks\n128\t\n129\t#### Test\n130\t- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n131\t\n132\t### Execution log\n133\t- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n134\t- 2026-11-25: review round 1 (rrn-d1680df442b23d12) requested changes. N1: in a scope with several specs (a milestone or a roadmap) each limit id is qualified by its spec as `<spec>#L<N>`, with the spec recorded, so a finding names exactly one sealed limit. N2: the reason must follow `Accepted because`, not only the marker. N3: an ADR names a limit only by the id as a word, so L10 does not name L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n135\t- 2026-11-25: review round 2 (rrn-256db7e6eea5a6e2) found that punctuation after the marker passed as a reason and a one-letter first word failed. The reason must now hold at least two words with letters (`hasAcceptedLimitReason`), with a test that fails with the rule mutated out.\n136\t- 2026-11-25: review round 3 (rrn-0af0d413761142d8) found that the marker matched inside a sentence, so 'Not accepted because ...' passed. The marker must now open a sentence (start of the text or after `.`, `;`, `:`, `!` or `?`), as the format `<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` states; the test fails with the boundary mutated out.\n137\t- 2026-11-25: review round 4 (rrn-721e2a4c9bd37e2e) found that an entry with no assumption, or with an empty reason followed by other prose, passed. The entry is now read structurally: an assumption of at least two words, the marker opening a sentence, and a reason of at least two words within that same sentence. Each rule has a test case that fails with it mutated out.\n138\t- 2026-11-25: review round 5 (rrn-9f6867b3c3e42377): N1, a whitespace-only reason of a `within-limit` finding is refused; N2, the ready check fails closed on an ADR directory or file it cannot read, keeping the absent-directory case. Both have tests that fail with them mutated out.\n139\t- 2026-11-25: review round 6 (rrn-a5b61489ac04873d) found the bundle's plan digest did not cover the sealed limits; it now does, omitted when empty so every other bundle keeps its digest. `TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits` checks both and fails with the field mutated out.\n140\t- 2026-11-25: review round 7 (rrn-1236e104ff346a2d): N2, the ready check reads an ADR only as a regular file, without following a link and within 1 MiB, so a link to an endless source fails instead of hanging; the test fails with the bounded read mutated out. N1 (low) was classified by the reviewer within L1: the check is a format gate, and the substance of a limit is judged in review.\n141\t- 2026-11-26: round 8 hit the Codex usage limit; by the maintainer's decision Claude Sonnet 5.5 reviews from here (a `claude-sonnet-5-5` profile in the delegation policy, the bundle resealed with the declared lead `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` so the review is `different-model`; vendor independence is not achieved). Its round (rrn-b874a0e13bc62075) recorded N6 within L1, the first live use of `limit`, and requested changes: N1, a wrapped entry now keeps its continuation lines; N2, fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations; N3, `review-brief/11` (not yet published, so its digest was re-recorded) states the exception to rule (i) and lists `limit` among the finding fields; N4, an ADR cites the spec only by its slug as a whole name; N5, governance outcomes count `within-limit` findings in their own bucket. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out. `gofmt` over the package's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n142\t\n143\t\n144\t### Results summary\n145\tThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n146\t\n147\t\n148\t### Requirement trace\n149\t- R1 [satisfied] test:TestAcceptedLimitsAreParsed, test:TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples, test:TestTheReviewPlanSealsTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestLimitsAcrossSpecsAreQualified, test:TestAnAcceptedLimitNeedsWordsAsItsReason\n150\t- R2 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefListsTheAcceptedLimits\n151\t- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAFindingWithinASealedLimitDoesNotBlockApproval, test:TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts\n152\t- R4 [satisfied] test:TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits\n153\t\n154\t\n155\t### Known gaps\n156\tNone.\n157\t\n158\t\n159\t## 7. Final Report\n160\t\n161\t### Delivered scope\n162\tSpecs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n163\t\n164\t\n165\t### Files and modules changed\n166\t`pose-mcp` (plan, bundle, conclusion, brief, lint), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n167\t\n168\t\n169\t### Validation executed\n170\t`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n171\t\n172\t\n173\t### Residual risks\n174\tL1 and L2 above.\n175\t\n176\t\n177\t### Follow-ups\n178\tNone.\n179\t\n180\t\n181\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"7506cd26-6d84-4a1f-bbac-82db6cbc78d6","timestamp":"2026-11-26T01:58:22.104Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2569977521/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md","content":"---\nslug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-23\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: \nremediates:\npriority: 4\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface: standard\ndelivers: capability:review-declared-threat-model\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nLet a spec declare the accepted residual limits of a guarantee it makes, and let the review brief treat findings inside those limits as follow-ups to classify, not blockers to chase.\n\n### Business value\nEach of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n\n### Constraints\nA declared limit never hides a defect inside the declared scope. The reviewer may dispute a limit; the dispute is a finding with its own classification. Declared limits are visible in the closeout and the attestation.\n\n### Non-goals\nLimiting the number of review rounds.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: A spec shall be able to declare `### Accepted limits` entries with stable ids (`L<N>`), each naming the assumption and why it is accepted.\n- R2: The review brief shall list the declared limits and instruct the reviewer to classify a finding that falls inside one as a follow-up naming the limit, or to dispute the limit explicitly.\n- R3: The conclusion and the attestation shall record which findings were classified under which limit.\n- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n\n### Non-functional\nExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n\n### Security\nNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n\n### Compatibility\nRecords and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp` (CLI and engine), the manual and its embedded copy.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n- modified: .pose/policy/delegation.json\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-declared-threat-model module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n\n\n### Accepted limits\n- L1: The limits are written by the implementer, so a limit can be broader than it should be. Accepted because the brief presents them as data to verify and a disputed limit is a blocking finding of its own.\n- L2: A spec edited after sealing changes its limits only through a new bundle. Accepted because the limits enter the plan digest, so an edit stales the review as any other change does.\n\n### API/contract changes\nA spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n\n\n### Data/storage changes\n`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n\n\n### Technical risks\nA limit could be used to wave a real defect through. It is the spec's own declaration, sealed into the plan digest and visible in the brief and the attestation; the reviewer may dispute it with a finding of its own, which then blocks as any finding does.\n\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [ ] Confirm the requirements against the current engine before starting.\n\n### Implementation\n- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n\n### Validation\n- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\nA finding inside a declared limit is neither an open finding nor an accepted risk: it gets its own disposition, `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity, requires a sealed limit and a reason, and records the limit's id. Accepted risks keep their severity bound; a limit is the spec's statement of scope, decided before the review.\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R1, R4\nThe limits are sealed in the plan and the bundle, and in the plan digest, so the brief and the conclusion read them from what was reviewed, never from the current spec; both fields are omitted when empty so no earlier plan or bundle changes digest.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 1 (rrn-d1680df442b23d12) requested changes. N1: in a scope with several specs (a milestone or a roadmap) each limit id is qualified by its spec as `<spec>#L<N>`, with the spec recorded, so a finding names exactly one sealed limit. N2: the reason must follow `Accepted because`, not only the marker. N3: an ADR names a limit only by the id as a word, so L10 does not name L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 2 (rrn-256db7e6eea5a6e2) found that punctuation after the marker passed as a reason and a one-letter first word failed. The reason must now hold at least two words with letters (`hasAcceptedLimitReason`), with a test that fails with the rule mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 3 (rrn-0af0d413761142d8) found that the marker matched inside a sentence, so 'Not accepted because ...' passed. The marker must now open a sentence (start of the text or after `.`, `;`, `:`, `!` or `?`), as the format `<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` states; the test fails with the boundary mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 4 (rrn-721e2a4c9bd37e2e) found that an entry with no assumption, or with an empty reason followed by other prose, passed. The entry is now read structurally: an assumption of at least two words, the marker opening a sentence, and a reason of at least two words within that same sentence. Each rule has a test case that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 5 (rrn-9f6867b3c3e42377): N1, a whitespace-only reason of a `within-limit` finding is refused; N2, the ready check fails closed on an ADR directory or file it cannot read, keeping the absent-directory case. Both have tests that fail with them mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 6 (rrn-a5b61489ac04873d) found the bundle's plan digest did not cover the sealed limits; it now does, omitted when empty so every other bundle keeps its digest. `TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits` checks both and fails with the field mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 7 (rrn-1236e104ff346a2d): N2, the ready check reads an ADR only as a regular file, without following a link and within 1 MiB, so a link to an endless source fails instead of hanging; the test fails with the bounded read mutated out. N1 (low) was classified by the reviewer within L1: the check is a format gate, and the substance of a limit is judged in review.\n- 2026-11-26: round 8 hit the Codex usage limit; by the maintainer's decision Claude Sonnet 5.5 reviews from here (a `claude-sonnet-5-5` profile in the delegation policy, the bundle resealed with the declared lead `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` so the review is `different-model`; vendor independence is not achieved). Its round (rrn-b874a0e13bc62075) recorded N6 within L1, the first live use of `limit`, and requested changes: N1, a wrapped entry now keeps its continuation lines; N2, fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations; N3, `review-brief/11` (not yet published, so its digest was re-recorded) states the exception to rule (i) and lists `limit` among the finding fields; N4, an ADR cites the spec only by its slug as a whole name; N5, governance outcomes count `within-limit` findings in their own bucket. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out. `gofmt` over the package's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n\n\n### Results summary\nThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestAcceptedLimitsAreParsed, test:TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples, test:TestTheReviewPlanSealsTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestLimitsAcrossSpecsAreQualified, test:TestAnAcceptedLimitNeedsWordsAsItsReason\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefListsTheAcceptedLimits\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAFindingWithinASealedLimitDoesNotBlockApproval, test:TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits\n\n\n### Known gaps\nNone.\n\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\nSpecs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp` (plan, bundle, conclusion, brief, lint), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n\n\n### Validation executed\n`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n\n\n### Residual risks\nL1 and L2 above.\n\n\n### Follow-ups\nNone.\n\n\n","numLines":181,"startLine":1,"totalLines":181}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01M6nhKLk6uVKmeD3iYEGREA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQiCna9KTQdkwrnMYcdW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R1Qfh7w2a7Z1dPHxH354mn","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat .pose/adr/2026-11-23-review-readiness-before-seal.md; git ls-files .pose/adr | grep -i -E 'threat|accepted|readiness'; git diff e778d632021b5b063af8ad8a2eb1811cdea4f214..92289d40 --stat -- pose-mcp POSE.md locales"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19686,"cache_read_input_tokens":27245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19686},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"34ffbd59-a3da-44d1-a273-5a45e670fa28","timestamp":"2026-11-26T01:58:22.897Z","request_id":"req_011ChQiCnNjcvQkKm3wkSJ3R","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01R1Qfh7w2a7Z1dPHxH354mn":{"command":"cat .pose/adr/2026-11-23-review-readiness-before-seal.md; git ls-files .pose/adr | grep -i -E 'threat|accepted|readiness'; git diff e778d632021b5b063af8ad8a2eb1811cdea4f214..92289d40 --stat -- pose-mcp POSE.md locales"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R1Qfh7w2a7Z1dPHxH354mn","type":"tool_result","content":"# ADR: Check review readiness before the seal and inside the run\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted on 2026-11-23 for `pose-review-readiness-before-seal`.\n\n## Context\n\nA delegated review round costs a seal, a run of about forty minutes and a conclusion. In the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks`, about seven were lost to refusals that existed before the reviewer started: `pose close` refused an approved scope for an undeclared delivery; the conclusion preflight refused drafts for a decision with no requirement basis, prefixed finding ids, prior findings outside the open ones, and criterion evidence outside its component. The reviewer could not see those rules applied to its own draft until the run had ended.\n\n## Decision\n\nMove each check to the earliest point that has its inputs, reusing the same predicate:\n\n- The seal of a new spec bundle refuses the declaration findings the delivery index holds at error severity for the scope (`undeclared-delivery` and the artifact finding `undeclared`), which `pose close` reports. Evidence findings stay at close. When a required criterion asks for structural mappings, it also refuses a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint, the namespace the preflight checks mappings against.\n- `pose review draft-check` runs the conclusion's draft parser, the round accounting and the attestation content checks over a draft, writing nothing. The content checks are the part of the bundle-attestation validation that does not depend on the run's signature or identity, extracted as one function both paths call.\n- A sealed checkout holds neither the bundle nor the run ledger, so the dispatch writes the bundle and the round's open findings to the run's private directory and names the file in `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT`. It also names its own executable in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, because the reviewer's shell may resolve another installed `pose`. `--draft -` reads standard input for read-only sandboxes. A run id selects its bundle.\n- The brief of the new template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run the check. Template texts are immutable, so `/7` stays for the runs briefed with it.\n\n## Consequences\n\nA scope that would fail at close now fails at seal, before any review cost. A reviewer can converge its draft inside one run. The draft check is advisory: the conclusion step stays the gate and still runs every check, including the signature and identity checks the draft check cannot run.\n\nResidual limits:\n- L1: the draft check judges against the context the dispatch wrote. A reviewer that edits that file only misleads itself, because the conclusion re-reads the sealed bundle and the ledger.\n- L2: the seal reads the delivery finding from the committed index. A stale index can miss a finding that a later `pose index` would add. `pose close` still refuses it, so the cost is a round, never a wrong closure.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- A per-instance cap on review rounds would stop the loop by approving unreviewed work.\n- Running the full `pose close` gate at seal would refuse scopes for evidence that only exists after the review.\n.pose/adr/2026-11-23-review-readiness-before-seal.md\n POSE.md                                            |  2 +-\n locales/pt-BR/POSE.md                              |  2 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go | 11 ++++-\n pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go                  |  5 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go          | 55 +++++++++++++++++-----\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go     | 13 +++++\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go    |  3 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go      |  5 ++\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go |  6 ++-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go             | 11 ++++-\n .../pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go     |  2 +-\n .../testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json    |  2 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md            |  2 +-\n .../internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md   |  2 +-\n 14 files changed, 95 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"c0ab5b36-fb4d-416b-b510-dd1e66e8fd48","timestamp":"2026-11-26T01:58:22.960Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# ADR: Check review readiness before the seal and inside the run\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted on 2026-11-23 for `pose-review-readiness-before-seal`.\n\n## Context\n\nA delegated review round costs a seal, a run of about forty minutes and a conclusion. In the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks`, about seven were lost to refusals that existed before the reviewer started: `pose close` refused an approved scope for an undeclared delivery; the conclusion preflight refused drafts for a decision with no requirement basis, prefixed finding ids, prior findings outside the open ones, and criterion evidence outside its component. The reviewer could not see those rules applied to its own draft until the run had ended.\n\n## Decision\n\nMove each check to the earliest point that has its inputs, reusing the same predicate:\n\n- The seal of a new spec bundle refuses the declaration findings the delivery index holds at error severity for the scope (`undeclared-delivery` and the artifact finding `undeclared`), which `pose close` reports. Evidence findings stay at close. When a required criterion asks for structural mappings, it also refuses a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint, the namespace the preflight checks mappings against.\n- `pose review draft-check` runs the conclusion's draft parser, the round accounting and the attestation content checks over a draft, writing nothing. The content checks are the part of the bundle-attestation validation that does not depend on the run's signature or identity, extracted as one function both paths call.\n- A sealed checkout holds neither the bundle nor the run ledger, so the dispatch writes the bundle and the round's open findings to the run's private directory and names the file in `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT`. It also names its own executable in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, because the reviewer's shell may resolve another installed `pose`. `--draft -` reads standard input for read-only sandboxes. A run id selects its bundle.\n- The brief of the new template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run the check. Template texts are immutable, so `/7` stays for the runs briefed with it.\n\n## Consequences\n\nA scope that would fail at close now fails at seal, before any review cost. A reviewer can converge its draft inside one run. The draft check is advisory: the conclusion step stays the gate and still runs every check, including the signature and identity checks the draft check cannot run.\n\nResidual limits:\n- L1: the draft check judges against the context the dispatch wrote. A reviewer that edits that file only misleads itself, because the conclusion re-reads the sealed bundle and the ledger.\n- L2: the seal reads the delivery finding from the committed index. A stale index can miss a finding that a later `pose index` would add. `pose close` still refuses it, so the cost is a round, never a wrong closure.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- A per-instance cap on review rounds would stop the loop by approving unreviewed work.\n- Running the full `pose close` gate at seal would refuse scopes for evidence that only exists after the review.\n.pose/adr/2026-11-23-review-readiness-before-seal.md\n POSE.md                                            |  2 +-\n locales/pt-BR/POSE.md                              |  2 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go | 11 ++++-\n pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go                  |  5 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go          | 55 +++++++++++++++++-----\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go     | 13 +++++\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go    |  3 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go      |  5 ++\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go |  6 ++-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go             | 11 ++++-\n .../pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go     |  2 +-\n .../testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json    |  2 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md            |  2 +-\n .../internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md   |  2 +-\n 14 files changed, 95 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01R1Qfh7w2a7Z1dPHxH354mn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQiCna9KTQdkwrnMYcdW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W4enfi1yQK27B3Usa17gWt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git diff e778d632021b5b063af8ad8a2eb1811cdea4f214..92289d40 -- pose-mcp POSE.md locales"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19686,"cache_read_input_tokens":27245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19686},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"0e6e5815-05eb-45d8-8b94-743557523a7f","timestamp":"2026-11-26T01:58:23.299Z","request_id":"req_011ChQiCnNjcvQkKm3wkSJ3R","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01W4enfi1yQK27B3Usa17gWt":{"command":"git diff e778d632021b5b063af8ad8a2eb1811cdea4f214..92289d40 -- pose-mcp POSE.md locales"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W4enfi1yQK27B3Usa17gWt","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (40.3KB). Full output saved to: /home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-2569977521-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/blpe7b5cm.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 989ca602..57f95eef 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -1075,7 +1075,7 @@ Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `po\n \n **Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n \n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"257635c5-e3d3-4e50-af55-8531661cc4c1","timestamp":"2026-11-26T01:58:23.358Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 989ca602..57f95eef 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -1075,7 +1075,7 @@ Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `po\n \n **Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n \n-**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit; `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n+**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, which may wrap over several lines (fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n \n **Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 7c49b041..f7b6d52d 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -1072,7 +1072,7 @@ Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicit\n \n **Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n \n-**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite; `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n+**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, que pode ocupar várias linhas (blocos de código e comentários HTML não são lidos como declarações); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n \n **Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux o envio roda o adaptador sob um shim de run de review, o mesmo executável reentrado, que é o subreaper da subárvore do run: depois que o adaptador termina, num timeout e quando o envio termina, ele encerra e recolhe todo processo que restou nessa subárvore, mesmo um que abriu sessão própria e limpou o ambiente, e relata os pids encerrados por um pipe que o revisor não herda; o envio os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza. Quem embute o motor sem ativar o shim recorre a encerrar os processos cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); nas outras plataformas Unix só o grupo de processos é encerrado, e no Windows o adaptador roda num job object com encerramento ao fechar, que o envio termina depois do run, num timeout e, pelo limite do job, quando o envio termina. O brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\nindex cacb146e..501ccf2d 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ func TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits(t *testing.T) {\n \tif code, out := run(); code == 0 || !strings.Contains(out, \"does not say why it is accepted\") {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unjustified limit passed: %d %s\", code, out)\n \t}\n-\twrite(\".pose/specs/limited/spec.md\", spec(\"\\n### Accepted limits\\n- L1: an escape. Accepted because it is rare.\\n- L2: another. Accepted because it is rarer.\\n\"))\n+\twrite(\".pose/specs/limited/spec.md\", spec(\"\\n### Accepted limits\\n- L1: an escape. Accepted because it is rare.\\n- L2: another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\\n\"))\n \t// Review round 1, N3: L10 does not name L1.\n \twrite(\".pose/adr/2026-11-25-limited.md\", \"# ADR\\n\\nFor `limited`. L10 and L2 are accepted.\\n\")\n \tif code, out := run(); code == 0 || !strings.Contains(out, \"accepted limit L1 is not named by the ADR\") {\n@@ -55,10 +55,17 @@ func TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tif err := os.Chmod(adr, 0o000); err != nil {\n \t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n \t\t}\n-\t\tdefer os.Chmod(adr, 0o644)\n \t\tif code, out := run(); code == 0 || !strings.Contains(out, \"cannot be read to check the accepted limits\") {\n \t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unreadable ADR was read as absent: %d %s\", code, out)\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif err := os.Chmod(adr, 0o644); err != nil {\n+\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\t// Review round 8, N4: an ADR about a longer slug does not cite this one.\n+\twrite(\".pose/adr/2026-11-25-limited.md\", \"# ADR\\n\\nFor `limited-other`. Nothing about this spec.\\n\")\n+\tif code, out := run(); code != 0 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an ADR citing a longer slug was taken to cite this spec: %d %s\", code, out)\n \t}\n \t// Review round 7, N2: an ADR that is a link is not followed.\n \tadr := filepath.Join(root, \".pose/adr/2026-11-25-limited.md\")\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\nindex 308531c4..a25b3f07 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n@@ -1197,6 +1197,7 @@ func acceptedLimitsMissingFromADRs(root, slug string, limits []posepkg.ReviewAcc\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\treturn []string{\"DoR: the ADRs cannot be read to check the accepted limits: \" + err.Error()}\n \t}\n+\tcitesSlug := regexp.MustCompile(`(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_-])` + regexp.QuoteMeta(slug) + `($|[^A-Za-z0-9_-])`)\n \tvar citing []string\n \tvar unreadable []string\n \tfor _, e := range entries {\n@@ -1208,7 +1209,9 @@ func acceptedLimitsMissingFromADRs(root, slug string, limits []posepkg.ReviewAcc\n \t\t\tunreadable = append(unreadable, \"DoR: ADR \"+e.Name()+\" cannot be read to check the accepted limits: \"+err.Error())\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif !strings.Contains(string(raw), slug) {\n+\t\t// The slug as a whole name: an ADR about a longer slug does not cite a\n+\t\t// shorter one it contains (review round 8, N4).\n+\t\tif !citesSlug.MatchString(string(raw)) {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tciting = append(citing, string(raw))\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\nindex ace87a99..2ea5736c 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n@@ -59,29 +59,60 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \tvar problems []string\n \tin := false\n \tseen := map[string]bool{}\n-\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(body, \"\\n\") {\n+\t// An entry may wrap over several lines: an indented or plain line that\n+\t// follows it continues it (review round 8, N1). Fenced blocks and HTML\n+\t// comments are not the spec's declarations, so examples there are never\n+\t// sealed (N2).\n+\ttype entry struct{ id, text string }\n+\tvar entries []entry\n+\tmalformed := func(line string) {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+quoteLedgerText(line)+\" is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n+\t}\n+\tfenced := false\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(stripHTMLComments(body), \"\\n\") {\n \t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"```\") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"~~~\") {\n+\t\t\tfenced = !fenced\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif fenced {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n-\t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, \"#\")), \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, \"#\")), \"Limites aceitos\")\n+\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, \"#\"))\n+\t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif !in || !strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") {\n+\t\tif !in || trimmed == \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tm := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed)\n-\t\tif m == nil {\n-\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+quoteLedgerText(trimmed)+\" is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n+\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") {\n+\t\t\tm := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed)\n+\t\t\tif m == nil {\n+\t\t\t\tmalformed(trimmed)\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{})\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2])})\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif len(entries) > 0 && entries[len(entries)-1].id != \"\" {\n+\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, e := range entries {\n+\t\tif e.id == \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif seen[m[1]] {\n-\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+m[1]+\" is declared twice\")\n+\t\tif seen[e.id] {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" is declared twice\")\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tseen[m[1]] = true\n-\t\tif !hasAcceptedLimitReason(m[2]) {\n-\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+m[1]+\" does not say why it is accepted ('Accepted because ...')\")\n+\t\tseen[e.id] = true\n+\t\tif !hasAcceptedLimitReason(e.text) {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" does not say why it is accepted ('Accepted because ...')\")\n \t\t}\n-\t\tlimits = append(limits, ReviewAcceptedLimit{ID: m[1], Text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2])})\n+\t\tlimits = append(limits, ReviewAcceptedLimit{ID: e.id, Text: e.text})\n \t}\n \treturn limits, problems\n }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\nindex f0219e94..4e18919b 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n@@ -149,3 +149,16 @@ func TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"the bundle plan digest does not cover the accepted limits: %s %s %s\", empty, one, other)\n \t}\n }\n+\n+// Review round 8, N1, N2: a wrapped entry keeps its continuation; fenced and\n+// commented examples are not sealed.\n+func TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples(t *testing.T) {\n+\tbody := \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape. Accepted because a shim covers it\\n  only on Linux and only for children.\\n\\n```\\n- L2: An example. Accepted because it is only shown.\\n```\\n<!--\\n- L3: A hidden one. Accepted because nobody sees it.\\n-->\\n\"\n+\tlimits, problems := ParseAcceptedLimits(body)\n+\tif len(problems) != 0 || len(limits) != 1 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"limits %+v problems %v\", limits, problems)\n+\t}\n+\tif !strings.HasSuffix(limits[0].Text, \"only on Linux and only for children.\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the continuation line was cut: %q\", limits[0].Text)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\nindex a570da47..1bfa0d68 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n@@ -138,7 +138,7 @@ func TestReviewBundlePreservesLegacyEvidenceOnlyForClosedScopes(t *testing.T) {\n func TestDeliverySurfaceMatchesRootModuleAndSubdirectoryTargets(t *testing.T) {\n \tset := ChangeSet{ID: \"cs-root\", Spec: \"root-spec\", Paths: []ObservedPath{{Action: \"modified\", Path: \"internal/pkg/foo.go\"}}}\n \tbase := BuildDeliveryIntegrity([]Spec{{Slug: \"root-spec\"}}, []ArtifactClaim{{Spec: \"root-spec\", Action: \"modified\", Path: \"internal/pkg/foo.go\"}}, []ChangeSet{set}, []string{\"internal/pkg/foo.go\"}, ArtifactPolicy{})\n-\t\n+\n \t// Target declared with module:internal/pkg\n \ttargetSub := DeliveryTarget{Spec: \"root-spec\", Ref: \"contract:my-contract\", Kind: \"contract\", Module: \"internal/pkg\", Profile: \"api-contract\", Entrypoint: \"internal/pkg/foo.go\"}\n \t// Target declared with module:.\n@@ -158,4 +158,3 @@ func TestDeliverySurfaceMatchesRootModuleAndSubdirectoryTargets(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected resolved paths for targets, got: %+v\", graph.Paths)\n \t}\n }\n-\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\nindex 050acbd8..e9541dcc 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n@@ -103,6 +103,7 @@ type GovernanceReviewDimensions struct {\n \tResolvedFindings         int `json:\"resolved_findings\"`\n \tWontFixFindings          int `json:\"wont_fix_findings\"`\n \tOpenFindings             int `json:\"open_findings\"`\n+\tWithinLimitFindings      int `json:\"within_limit_findings\"`\n \tStaleObserved            int `json:\"stale_observed\"`\n \tStaleUnknown             int `json:\"stale_unknown\"`\n }\n@@ -451,6 +452,10 @@ func (s Store) GovernanceOutcomes(query GovernanceOutcomesQuery) (*GovernanceOut\n \t\t\t\treport.Reviews.WontFixFindings++\n \t\t\tcase \"open\", \"changes-requested\":\n \t\t\t\treport.Reviews.OpenFindings++\n+\t\t\tcase \"within-limit\":\n+\t\t\t\t// A finding inside a declared accepted limit (spec\n+\t\t\t\t// pose-review-declared-threat-model, review round 8, N5).\n+\t\t\t\treport.Reviews.WithinLimitFindings++\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n \t\tif findingInAttempt {\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\nindex ff0a416f..b43d17f9 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n@@ -77,7 +77,7 @@ func TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts(t *testing.T) {\n \tbundleRaw, _ := json.Marshal(bundle)\n \twriteGovernanceFile(t, root, \".pose/review-bundles/\"+bundle.BundleID+\".json\", string(bundleRaw)+\"\\n\")\n \n-\tatt := ReviewAttestation{SchemaVersion: ReviewBundleSchemaVersion, BundleID: bundle.BundleID, BundleDigest: bundle.BundleDigest, Reviewer: \"agent:reviewer\", Decision: \"changes-requested\", Criteria: []ReviewCriterion{{ID: \"scope\", Disposition: \"not-applicable\", Rationale: \"fixture\"}}, Findings: []ReviewFinding{{ID: \"f1\", Severity: \"low\", Disposition: \"accepted-risk\"}}, AttestedAt: \"2026-09-19T00:01:00Z\"}\n+\tatt := ReviewAttestation{SchemaVersion: ReviewBundleSchemaVersion, BundleID: bundle.BundleID, BundleDigest: bundle.BundleDigest, Reviewer: \"agent:reviewer\", Decision: \"changes-requested\", Criteria: []ReviewCriterion{{ID: \"scope\", Disposition: \"not-applicable\", Rationale: \"fixture\"}}, Findings: []ReviewFinding{{ID: \"f1\", Severity: \"low\", Disposition: \"accepted-risk\"}, {ID: \"f2\", Severity: \"high\", Disposition: \"within-limit\", Limit: \"L1\", Rationale: \"inside L1\"}}, AttestedAt: \"2026-09-19T00:01:00Z\"}\n \tatt.AttestationID = reviewAttestationID(att)\n \tattRaw, _ := json.Marshal(att)\n \twriteGovernanceFile(t, root, \".pose/review-attestations/\"+att.AttestationID+\".json\", string(attRaw)+\"\\n\")\n@@ -96,7 +96,9 @@ func TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts(t *testing.T) {\n \tif report.Reviews.BundlesObserved != 1 || report.Reviews.AttestationsObserved != 2 || report.Reviews.ChangesRequested != 1 || report.Reviews.Approved != 1 {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"review dimensions = %+v\", report.Reviews)\n \t}\n-\tif report.Reviews.NotApplicable != 1 || report.Reviews.AcceptedRisk != 1 || report.Reviews.FindingsObserved != 1 || report.Reviews.InterventionsObserved != 1 {\n+\t// A finding within a declared limit has its own bucket (spec\n+\t// pose-review-declared-threat-model, review round 8, N5).\n+\tif report.Reviews.NotApplicable != 1 || report.Reviews.AcceptedRisk != 1 || report.Reviews.WithinLimitFindings != 1 || report.Reviews.FindingsObserved != 2 || report.Reviews.InterventionsObserved != 1 {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"intervention dimensions = %+v\", report.Reviews)\n \t}\n \tif report.Attempts.JudgmentAttempts != 2 || report.Attempts.FirstApprovedUnits != 1 || report.Attempts.SupersededRelations != 1 || report.Reviews.StaleUnknown != 1 {\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\nindex ce828902..debc921f 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n@@ -143,6 +143,11 @@ func briefRulesText(template string, bundle ReviewBundle) string {\n \tif reviewBriefFromAllNotApplicable(template) {\n \t\ttext += \" (l) a draft that dispenses with every required criterion as `not-applicable` is refused: a scope whose whole plan is inapplicable is not reviewed by it;\"\n \t}\n+\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateAcceptedLimits {\n+\t\t// The exception to (i) for a finding inside a declared limit, which\n+\t\t// the engine applies (review round 8, N3).\n+\t\ttext += \" (m) the one exception to (i): a follow-up whose `limit` names an accepted limit listed above is recorded within that limit, needs no `action`, `owner` or `review_by` and does not block an approval whatever its severity; its limit must be listed and its `summary` says why it falls inside;\"\n+\t}\n \treturn text + \" (j) the `evidence` of a `passed` criterion is